Description: Let a hidden window with a known monitor and size update its actor before it is shown.
 Mutter freezes the updates of a Wayland window until the window is shown for the first time, since only then did
 it know which monitor the window is on, and thus its size. While they are frozen, the window's actor neither
 follows the window's geometry nor shows the content the client committed, so it stays 0x0. A window which is
 hidden from the start, like one a session restores to another workspace, is shown only when that workspace is
 activated, and until then previews of it, like the overview's workspace thumbnails, show nothing where it is.
 .
 A hidden window which is placed or not floating already has its monitor and size: mutter places a hidden floating
 window as soon as it has a buffer so that previews of hidden windows know where they are, a restored floating
 window is placed from its stored state, and a maximized, tiled or fullscreen window has the size the constraints
 give it on its monitor, which for a restored one is the monitor of its stored state. The updates of a Wayland
 window are now frozen only until it is shown, or while it is hidden, until it is placed or not floating, and the
 frozen state is synced after a window is hidden. Windows shown when they map are unchanged.
 .
 The tests restore-other-workspace, restore-maximized-other-workspace and restore-tiled-other-workspace restore a
 window, floating, or maximized or tiled on a second monitor, to the second workspace while the first one is active,
 and check that the window is hidden, on its monitor, and that its actor has the position and size of the window's
 buffer, which fails without the change, as the actor is 0x0. They then activate the second workspace and check that
 the window is shown with the same geometry and its actor following it.
Author: Mitar
Last-Update: 2026-11-16
diff --git a/src/core/window.c b/src/core/window.c
